  "text": "I added memoization to `fib.js`, but I couldn't run it: both `node` commands needed approval, so it hasn't been tested.\n\n```js\nconst memo = new Map();\n\nfunction fib(n) {\n  if (n < 2) return n;\n  if (!memo.has(n)) memo.set(n, fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2));\n  return memo.get(n);\n}\n```\n\nEach value is now computed once, so the running time is linear in `n` (before, it grew exponentially). The exported `fib` and the command-line behaviour are unchanged. To check it, run `node fib.js 90`. It should print `2880067194370816000` almost instantly.\n\n- **Skipped a way to clear the cache:** results stay in memory for the life of the process. Add one only if memory becomes a problem.\n- **Deep recursion:** a very large first call, around 10,000 or more, may hit a stack overflow. For those sizes, switch to a simple loop.\n- **Precision limit:** above `n = 78` the result exceeds JavaScript's safe integer range (`Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER`), so results are rounded. The 90 example comes out as `2880067194370816000`, while the exact value ends in `120`. Use `BigInt` if you need exact values."
